# Non-membership of j(qᵖ) in the level-M divisor-expansion field

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.jqN_prime_not_mem_full` (`f53dbbb6-357c-503b-9993-e09764b6d02c`),
by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `4734660d-014f-5776-b9b9-7096efa42a11`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $M$ be a nonzero natural number and $p$ a prime not dividing $M$. Work inside the field
$\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$, where `jq` is the series $q^{-1}$ times the
power series `jNumQ` (the integral numerator series of the $j$-invariant, with coefficients mapped
into $\mathbb{Q}$), and for a nonzero $N$ the series `jqN N` is the image of `jq` under the ring
homomorphism `qExpand ℚ N`, which multiplies all exponents by $N$, i.e. the substitution $q \mapsto
q^N$. Assume that for every nonzero divisor $d$ of $M$ both of the following hold: the degree of
$\mathbb{Q}(jq)\bigl(\mathtt{jqN } d\bigr)$ over $\mathbb{Q}(jq)$ equals `dedekindPsi d`, defined as
$\sum_{e \mid d,\ e \text{ squarefree}} d/e$; and the field $\mathbb{Q}(jq, \mathtt{jqN } d)$
generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the two series coincides with the field generated over $\mathbb{Q}$
by all expansions $\mathtt{jqN } e$ with $e$ a nonzero divisor of $d$. The conclusion is that `jqN
p`, the series $j(q^p)$, does not lie in the field generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the expansions
$\mathtt{jqN } d$ for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $M$.

This is the base step of the non-membership tower used in the purely algebraic computation of
$[\mathbb{Q}(j) (j_N) : \mathbb{Q}(j)] = \psi(N)$, equivalently of the irreducibility of the modular
polynomial $\Phi_N$ over $\mathbb{Q}(j)$ and of the description of the function field of $X_0(N)$.
It is cited in the inductive construction of irreducible modular polynomial data and in the
construction of Atkin–Lehner type automorphisms at a prime away from the level.

